WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.319
So unless you've avoided news of all kinds
for the last four or five days, you probably

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:11.730
know that things in the United States have
gotten extremely intense on top of a global

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:17.100
hundred year pandemic corona virus, where
the United States has been one of the worst

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.900
hit countries, thanks in part to our failed
federal leadership.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:29.509
We have seen the tragedy of George Floyd being
killed by police in Minnesota, set off a string

00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:32.250
of extreme civil unrest.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:38.930
And this includes examples of just about everything
you can imagine, both by protesters and by

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.470
those who are pretending to be protesters,
but actually have nefarious intentions and

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.650
by police as well.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:54.560
And meanwhile, we are completely lacking anything
that would be called leadership from the Oval

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.020
Office, with Donald Trump doing everything
from calling for pro Trump protesters to gather

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.220
at the White House on Saturday night, which
they didn't.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:09.010
All the way to a tweeting about essentially
release the hounds talking about Secret Service's

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.050
vicious dogs.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:14.620
That would be unleashed on protesters who
breached the fence at the White House up to

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:19.740
and including of flying to Florida and taking
credit for a Space X launch of a Falcon nine

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.250
rocket carrying two astronauts to the International
Space Station while retweeting videos of himself

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.280
talking about all of it Saturday as protests
raged and buildings burned.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:39.200
And I really spent a lot of time this weekend
thinking about what's the primary story here?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.560
What's like the main analysis that has to
be done?

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.820
What's the lens through which we need to understand
what's happening in our country right now?

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.130
Because I wanted to come to you and give you
that point of view.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.180
Here's really what this is about.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.430
And the conclusion I came to is that there
is no one lens.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:05.740
Things are more complicated than this is really
what's going on.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.390
And I know that there are a ton of hot takes
about this.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:14.120
If you look at Twitter lately, if you look
at cable news, everybody claims to hold the

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.660
real key.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.760
They claim to say this is the truth filter
through which this should all be seen.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.690
I'm here to tell you I don't have such a lens
for you because I believe any such lens is

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:26.690
too simplistic.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.060
Now, if you want that simplistic analysis,
there are plenty available.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.890
There's no question about it.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:38.510
But to some degree, we sometimes are blinded
by narrative.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:44.160
And in some cases, if all you have is a hammer,
everything looks like a nail.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:52.459
Meaning if you want to see every issue as
being related to one problem, you're able

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:57.380
to find a way to do that, even if it's not
really the right or most robust analysis.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.690
So I'm going to give you some examples and
then we'll get to what I think is a reasonable

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.110
analysis and way to think about all of this.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:07.849
So one storyline we're hearing and we've heard
it from a lot of different people is this

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:09.989
is merely about race.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.569
This is about white cops killing black people.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:16.960
This is about white cops oppressing black
protesters.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.880
This is about a fundamentally racist justice
system.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.610
It's about a fundamental, fundamentally racist
society.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.300
That's what the lens is.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:32.930
Now, of course, rage at race is a massive
component that is causing understandable rage

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.480
among a lot of people.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.410
But that analysis doesn't tell you everything
that you need to know.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:45.690
Our economic system at its core, for example,
functions to stymie and suppress real change

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:46.690
and activism.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:47.690
And it goes way beyond.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:53.239
Race has racial implications, of course, but
you miss that if you make this only about

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:54.500
race.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:00.190
Much of that economic system is certainly
rooted in supremacy, is along racial lines,

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.050
but a race only analysis falls short.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.800
A lot of the hot takes we're seeing are only
about race.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:14.100
Another perspective that is heavily represented
is that this is really about capitalism, right?

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:21.579
Any capitalist system is inevitably racist
and inevitably leads to this degree of social

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:27.289
unrest, arson, looting, property damage, police
violence against protesters, protester, violence

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:31.470
against police, protester and protester violence,
protesters versus looters.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:36.759
That the idea is that is because of a capitalist
system.

00:04:36.759 --> 00:04:41.610
Making this simply about capitalism ignores
the countless social democracies.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:42.610
Right.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.930
Which that's a form of capitalism is regulated
capitalism that are some of the places where

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:52.150
these issues of inequality and racial tensions
have been best dealt with around the world.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:59.819
Some of the best examples, in fact, of functioning
societies with reduced income inequality reduce.

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:02.819
Racial inequality, social safety nets.

00:05:02.819 --> 00:05:04.339
Trust in institutions.

00:05:04.339 --> 00:05:06.030
Less corruption.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.020
They are regulated versions of capitalism.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.089
So I believe that the capitalism analysis
also fails.

00:05:13.089 --> 00:05:18.949
Another lens that's being presented as the
lens is simply about police brutality.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:19.949
Meaning?

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:24.229
This is about police brutality as what killed
George Floyd.

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:27.470
This is about police brutality as the problem
in the protests.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.680
In other words, if it weren't for police brutality.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.340
George Floyd would be alive if George Floyd
were alive.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.479
We wouldn't be seeing these protests.

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:39.189
We at the protests are also just seeing further
police brutality.

00:05:39.189 --> 00:05:43.810
Now, there is no shortage of police brutality
at the protests.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.259
What happened to George Floyd is unconscionable.

00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:52.309
But this is about so much more than that,
including economic and racial issues.

00:05:52.309 --> 00:05:56.270
It's about the militarization of law enforcement
in the United States.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.529
And there are people making it only about
that.

00:05:58.529 --> 00:06:01.520
It's not only about that, but that's that's
a part of it.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.149
When you militarize law enforcement, you create
overly aggressive policing.

00:06:07.149 --> 00:06:11.819
You can argue that that played a role in George
Floyds Death.

00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:16.039
Militarization is what led police in riot
gear in dozens of cities over the weekend

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:22.869
to use tear gas and rubber bullets and to
see media and random bystanders and even people

00:06:22.869 --> 00:06:27.830
peacefully protesting, which is, of course,
their right to do horribly injured by police.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.810
So militarization of law enforcement is another
huge problem in the US.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:36.569
We've covered it, but it is not the only lens
that we need to be using.

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:37.819
So here's the reality.

00:06:37.819 --> 00:06:41.300
When you think about this, and we often want
things to be black and white, we want to say

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.069
this is what this is really about.

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:48.589
The reality is this is a country of three
hundred and thirty one million people.

00:06:48.589 --> 00:06:50.629
We have examples of everything you can imagine.

00:06:50.629 --> 00:06:54.759
We have dozens of different problems that
relate to what we are now seeing.

00:06:54.759 --> 00:07:01.649
Just this weekend, we saw sickening violence
done by police to protesters, including, for

00:07:01.649 --> 00:07:06.379
example, an elderly man with a cane shoved
onto the ground by police.

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:10.409
And we saw police and I believe it was Michigan
show up until the protesters.

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:12.409
I'm putting down my weapons.

00:07:12.409 --> 00:07:13.759
I'm with you.

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:15.639
Let's march together.

00:07:15.639 --> 00:07:21.259
We saw protesters burn entire buildings and
brutalized business owners, including middle

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:22.259
aged women.

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:29.249
And yet we also saw protesters guard a police
officer who was separated from his squad on

00:07:29.249 --> 00:07:30.659
Saturday night.

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:32.759
And this is the risk about the.

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:36.610
Out of context sharing of pictures and video
on social media.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.559
Ten seconds from one protest and 15 from another.

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:41.990
And and I actually did some of this over the
weekend.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:44.339
And I don't know that it was the best thing.

00:07:44.339 --> 00:07:49.919
Not only does it lack immediate context, it
lacks broader context about all of the issues

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:54.679
that have gotten us to where we are now, including,
by the way, a complete lack of leadership

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:55.679
from the president.

00:07:55.679 --> 00:07:58.309
And we're going to talk about that later,
which is why I'm not digging into it too much

00:07:58.309 --> 00:07:59.309
right here.

00:07:59.309 --> 00:08:07.860
So my message is what we are seeing is mostly
about systems and it is less about individuals.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.611
You're seeing a ton of debates about what
portion of police are good versus bad, which

00:08:13.611 --> 00:08:15.610
is very fraught terminology anyway.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.330
Good, good and bad.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.120
What portion of protesters have good or bad
intentions?

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.240
What portion of protesters are grassroots
members of the community versus out of town?

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.830
Astroturf is, as has been a growing narrative
over the weekend, used in part to discredit

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:37.310
the real and justified anger that protesters
have about what's going on in their communities.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:42.600
The problem is, if you just argue about that,
if you spend your time on those questions,

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.830
well, eighty 82 percent of the protesters
are good or whatever you might say.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.490
You're not really digging into the systemic
elements at play.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:56.390
If we figure out it's 85 percent of the protesters
that are good instead of 80, then what?

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:58.510
It's good fodder for corporate media.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.290
It does little for anybody else.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:06.100
So I don't pretend to have one single unifying
theory about this.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.980
This is what it's really about.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.850
And at the exclusion of other things, that's
not something you're going to hear from me.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.910
But what I can tell you for sure is that you
won't understand any of this if you don't

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.290
understand the context.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.440
And that's where a lot of the local news coverage
of the protests has failed, which is a complete

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:22.500
lack of context.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.850
Oh, somebody looted a Cheesecake Factory and
they came out with the cheesecake.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.740
That's coverage that we saw.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.900
Doesn't really tell us anything about what's
going on.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:38.630
So racialized policing, combined with a two
tiered justice system for rich and poor, a

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:44.190
system that focuses on street crime at the
exclusion of white collar crime, combined

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:49.230
with an economic system that doesn't work
for a huge portion of the country.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:54.850
In addition to right now having nearly 25
percent unofficial unemployment in the midst

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:59.020
of a 100 year pandemic, in addition to a complete
absence of.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:03.030
Federal leadership from a president who tweeted
all weekend from his bunker at the White House,

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:07.710
combined with corporate media that really
is seeking to foment division rather than

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:12.020
find solutions, doesn't it's not a good recipe.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:17.920
And then you add to that militarized law enforcement
that seems to always have money, despite we

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.390
supposedly lacking our supposed lack of money
for health care and education.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:23.800
It's all of that stuff together.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.280
Now, I want to be really clear.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:33.050
This is not like a social justice intersectionality
analysis of race and gender and rule.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:38.120
It's not that what it is, is an understanding
that we have systems in our country that are

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:41.530
bigger than any one protest or any one police
officer.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:46.890
And only with that multi focus perspective
can you really get anything that we would

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.470
call an all encompassing analysis.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.820
Let me give you another example of a false
debate.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:58.540
Riots are good versus riots or bad or riots
are effective versus the riots are ineffective

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:01.250
or they are criminal or they are heroic.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:06.170
First of all, a ton of this debate is actually
semantic in terms of some people are arguing

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:08.840
over whether riots are good or bad.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:12.570
Others are arguing over what counts as a riot
versus a protest.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:14.350
There's racialization of the language.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.220
There's a lack of context, etc..

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:24.270
But what you really have to understand is
you can simultaneously say I'm against violence,

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:25.430
both against people.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:30.650
And property, I believe is it is unlikely
that the violence will achieve much.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:35.880
And yet, at the same time, I understand that
people have had it and I get what's going

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:37.560
on right.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:43.130
Someone's son is killed by police and then
a window gets smashed.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:50.350
It's hard to feel like these things are equivalent
in any way without needing to endorse property

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.350
damage.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:54.860
We can understand that people have absolutely
had it.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.130
People are sick and tired of the status quo.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:03.980
And while I might not think we will achieve
change by breaking windows, I understand and

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:09.990
sympathize with the absolute anger and disgust
with these systems that protesters have.

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:15.350
Now, I have to tell you the argument that
the unrest has accomplished nothing.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.930
I don't know if it's a good argument.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:17.930
You all know that.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:21.530
I have always said I favor legal put protests.

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:26.520
I'm against violence again, including against
most forms of property crime.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.720
Over the weekend, we saw the officer who killed
George Floyd, Derek Schavan, arrested.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:39.760
We saw Fox News Sunday devote an hour to the
protests, to George Floyd and to how coronavirus

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.860
disproportionately affects communities of
color with guests.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:44.350
Cornell West.

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:50.640
I don't know that were it not for the national
unrest, we would have seen that be the Fox

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:51.810
News Sunday show.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:52.810
I don't know.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:57.850
I think that what's what we're seeing played
a role in what Fox News even decided to do

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:59.430
on Sunday.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.650
Would we have seen Derek Schavan get arrested?

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:06.160
Would we potentially be seeing the other three
officers get arrested were it not for this

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:07.480
degree of unrest?

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:08.950
I don't know is the question.

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:14.760
It's less clear to me that this has accomplished
nothing, and that is separate from whether

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.640
these are tactics that I condone.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:20.170
Last thing I want to be really clear here.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:25.080
This is not like some Kumbayah segment where
everyone's thoughts are equally valid and

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.470
everyone is equally responsible for what's
going on.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:28.860
That's not what this is.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:34.240
But what we need to understand is that the
word privilege is often thrown around.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:35.420
You're privileged.

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:39.630
If you say cops are mostly good and protect
you because a lot of people live in places

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:44.450
where the cops aren't primarily there to protect
you, or at least it certainly doesn't feel

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:45.450
that way.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:50.200
If you're part of certain communities or you're
privileged to be against property crime during

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:56.050
protests because you live somewhere safe and
the systems work for you, and you can't possibly

00:13:56.050 --> 00:14:00.660
imagine what it's like to actually be in the
position that a lot of the protesters are

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.710
in in some way.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.130
All of those allegations are correct.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:09.790
And yet this is not so much about individual
privilege.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:15.590
It's, again, systems that aren't working for
large swaths of people.

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:19.070
And that gets me to my last thought in history.

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:24.920
We've seen unrest lead to movements which
lead to activism, which will often enter the

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:30.540
political and legal space, which at some point
will end up being decided by a judge somewhere.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:36.110
Look at Brown v. Board of Education, the landmark
decision decision that said states can't segregate

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.080
schools by race.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.250
That was in 1954.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:43.220
It started in 1951 in Kansas.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:47.920
It involved three years of activism, protests,
consensus building.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:52.950
And eventually it was a Supreme Court decision
under Earl Warren that decided nine to zero

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:54.280
on the court.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:58.890
Schools can't racially segregate as we figure
out what needs to happen next.

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:03.260
Do not miss the forest for the trees that
may come one, two or three years down the

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:04.260
line.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.550
If what is going on now will lead to change.

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:10.690
It may come down to who is on the Supreme
Court in one, two or three years.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:14.182
I don't even have to tell you what that means
about a decision we all have to make five

00:15:14.182 --> 00:15:14.230
months from now, because you all know exactly
what that decision is.

